In comments filed last month, the Energy Service Providers Assn. (ESPA), SouthStar En- 
ergy Services/Georgia Natural Gas, and SCANA Energy Marketing all question the proposal’s 
legality. 
ESPA, representing Shell Energy Services, The New Power Co. and PS Energy Group/ 
GasKey, conceded, though, that more still needs to be done to remedy the billing situation in 
Georgia. 
“Make no mistake about it — marketers have not done enough to ensure that all cus- 
tomers receive timely and accurate bills for services rendered since the random assignment 
process which concluded in September 1999,” ESPA said. “Marketers who have experienced 
problems are actively working to resolve those problems — and already having 
success.” 
But ESPA said it is “gravely concerned about the legality, wisdom and practical effect of 
the proposed rules upon the Georgia natural gas market.” The group said the NOPR was more 
likely to compound billing problems and urged the PSC to resist a proposal “which will do 
nothing to improve the technical problems while at the same time encourage customer fraud 
and compound marketplace confusion. 
“Increasing marketer costs by forgiving debts owed by customers will shift costs to other 
customers in the form of higher rates,” ESPA said. “Such a result would be government intervention 
at its worst, contrary to the intent of the Georgia Natural Gas Competition and Deregulation 
Act.” ESPA added that the 90-day rule was contrary to Georgia law since the commission 
has no right to regulate marketers’ sales rates and that the rule would essentially set the rate of 
“untimely” bills to zero. 
The proposal also violates the Takings Clause of the Fifth Amendment and the Contracts 
Clause of the U.S. Constitution, ESPA argued. 
??????????????????????????????????             
Just taking the proposal public has resulted in market confusion, ESPA added. Many cus- 
tomers are already asking the commission and marketers whether they need to pay their gas 
bills, the association said. If the proposal is approved, customers could commit fraud by just 
throwing away their bill for three months and claiming it was never received, the marketer 
group warned. 
The “wrath of a free market” is already enough incentive for marketers to solve any re- 
maining problems, ESPA concluded. 
SouthStar/Georgia Natural, the partnership of AGL Resources, Dynegy and Piedmont 
Natural Gas, made similar arguments regarding the 90-day rule. 
“It would be an unnecessary diversion from the central commitment of resolving billing 
issues if marketers and the commission were forced to litigate over the legality of the proposed 
rules,” Georgia Natural said. In its comments, the marketer proposed its own set of rules addressing 
timely bills, accuracy and limitations on late charges and disconnections. 
Georgia Natural said the PSC’s proposal was “fraught with legal, technical and prac- 
tical difficulties. Increasing the complexity of bills will do little to improve billing problems.” 
Recently, Georgia Natural has received some bad press from its apparent back-billing of 
base charges for some thousands of customers in Georgia. Georgia Natural Gas President Steve 
Gunther did not return calls for comment on the back-billing issue. 
Commissioner Baker noted last week that the PSC is still awaiting an expected opinion on 
the NOPR from the Georgia Attorney General, which could affect the NOPR as well.  SGS 
