Avista seeks growth with new leadership team 
A 
vista has reaffirmed its growth strategy and said it would continue to focus on its subsidiaries 
as sources of shareholder value, even with its newly appointed leadership team. 
“We will continue our goal of value creation and emphasize this as our number one objec- 
tive,” said Larry Stanley, who replaced Tom Matthews as non-executive chairman of Avista’s 
board. Matthews resigned as chairman, effective last Friday. “We also remain diligent in our 
work to strengthen our utility business — the backbone of our company,” Stanley added. 
Stanley will finish out Matthews’ term until it expires in May 2001 (GD 10/23). He is the 
chairman and CEO of Spokane-based Empire Bolt & Screw. 
In addition, Erik Anderson was appointed to the board of directors to fill the vacancy 
created by Matthews’ departure. Anderson is the CEO of Seattle-based Matthew G. Norton. 
Gary Ely has been named Avista president and CEO. He previously served as executive 
vice president. 
The board also named two new corporate vice presidents: Scott Morris, who is president 
of Avista Utilities, and Kelly Norwood, who serves as vice president and general manger of 
Avista Utilities’ energy resources department.                                   
