Crosstex acquires Tejas’ Gulf Coast system 
C 
rosstex Energy has acquired the 484-mile Gulf Coast Pipeline System as part of its purchase 
of Tejas Gas Transmission, the company said last Friday. The deal closed Sept. 15 for 
an undisclosed price. 
The purchase of the system is “part of an aggressive growth strategy being pursued with new 
funding received in May of this year from Yorktown Energy Partners,” said Crosstex President 
and CEO Jim Wales. “It is a big step in our plan to become a major midstream pipeline company.” 
The system is an intrastate pipeline in Texas, running from Refugio County in the south, 
along the Gulf Coast, to the Brazos River in Fort Bend County near Houston. Included in the 
system are two supply pipeline laterals off the mainline, gathering gas from about 500 wellheads 
and six plant tailgates. In addition, three laterals deliver gas to industrial and utility 
consumers along the Gulf Coast. 
Current throughput on the system, Crosstex said, is about 120 million cfd. 
“The purchase of the Gulf Coast System provides Crosstex with a large footprint on the 
Gulf Coast of Texas,” said Jim Wales, Crosstex executive vice president. He said the company 
intends to enhance its producer relationships “by expanding the upstream/field services we 
provide to include gathering, compression, treating and processing.”                
