SJG boosts rates by 19% 
South Jersey Gas (SJG) can increase its 
rates by 19%, effective immediately, the New 
Jersey Board of Public Utilities (BPU) ruled last 
week. Initially, South Jersey filed an emergency 
request for an increase of up to 39.7% to cover 
the rising cost of gas. 
Typical residential customers will see their 
monthly bills increase from $85.69 to $101.96. 
Starting in December, South Jersey can 
adjust its overall customer bill upwards or downwards 
by 2% each month through April 2001 to 
reflect market conditions, the BPU said. “As soon 
as it is possible to reduce this rate, we will file to 
do so,” said Charles Biscieglia, South Jersey Gas 
chairman, president and CEO. 
The BPU also ordered South Jersey to 
make a filing no later than Dec. 1 that shows the 
company’s latest pricing projections, gas recovery 
balances and hedging plans or other gas 
purchasing strategies.                 
