AGL back on the offensive in the earnings race 
J 
ust over a year after Atlanta Gas Light completely exited the merchant business, its parent 
company, AGL Resources, already stands to enjoy financial success as it overcomes the 
financial hurdles erected by the unbundling process. 
“Their problems look like they’re pretty well behind them here. They look like they’re 
moving forward quite well,” said Michael Garvey, an analyst at CIBC World Markets. 
On Oct. 1, 1999, Atlanta Gas Light stopped all gas sales — one year ahead of its own 
schedule (GD 9/24/99) — after the Georgia legislature placed unbundling on an accelerated 
track. As a result, the company faced unexpected problems, mostly because the utility unbundled 
before it could sufficiently cut costs. 
“The theory is that in deregulation, your costs need to fall at the same rate as your rev- 
enues are falling, because as customers leave your system, obviously your revenues are going 
away so you want your costs to go away in the same increments,” said Zach Wagner, an analyst 
at Edward Jones. “Unfortunately, that didn’t happen for AGL.” 
But during the past 12 months, the company has been cutting costs at all levels of its 
business. AGL has continued growing its customer base and making decisions on nonstrategic 
assets, such as propane and wholesale marketing, said A.G. Edwards analyst 
Michael Heim. 
For the quarter ending Sept. 30, AGL reported net income, excluding one-time items, 
of $14.9 million, compared with $4.8 million for the same period in 1999. The fourthquarter 
numbers resulted in total earnings for the past 12 months, excluding one-time 
items, of $1.24 per share, an increase of 33¢ over the 91¢ per share from operations for 
fiscal year 1999. 
“I think the company really in the last couple of quarters has started to make some good 
ground on, not only handling those problems, but getting back on the offensive and setting 
their new strategic direction going forward,” Heim said. 
Last month, AGL entered the Virginia gas market by completing the purchase of Virginia 
Natural Gas (GD 10/10). AGL is considering expanding its customer base further in the future. 
“But we will only grow as fast as we feel that we can absorb other franchises,” AGL Resources 
President and CEO Paula Rosput told Gas Daily. 
“There is not a single aspect of our business that hasn’t been open to complete exami- 
nation and process re-engineering,” said Rosput. “It’s been an arduous task, but everything 
had something in it that could be made better, and we’re not at the end of that process 
yet. But we’ve had a great year of trying to really change our business practices,” she 
added. 
“[Deregulation] was an enormous shock to the system, and it was very painful to travel 
through, but we believe that we are so much better a company today for the experience, not just 
in terms of being able to restore our earnings, but we have an employee base today that is so 
much more resilient and creative than we had prior to deregulation,” Rosput said. 
Meanwhile, AGL is in the process of seeking to build “the largest contiguous ring of 
conduit for fiber optics that’s ever been built in Atlanta,” Rosput said. The company will use a 
combination of expired gas lines and new facilities to transport fiber, and then lease out the 
space to telecommunications carriers. 
In the next few weeks, AGL will begin to announce a series of partnerships with commu- 
nication carriers, Rosput said.                                               
