Kan. purchase contract requirements amended 
T 
he Kansas Corporation Commission (KCC) amended its requirements for gas purchase 
contracts in the state — the first changes to the rules in 23 years. The KCC order results 
from a proceeding, opened more than a year ago, for fixing the general policies for the 
purchase of gas by local distribution companies (LDCs). 
The new order, dated Nov. 1, applies to all gas purchase contracts beginning Dec. 1. Peti- 
tions for reconsideration of the order can be made within 15 days of the order, although KCC’s 
Rosemary Foreman said no such petitions are anticipated. 
According to the commission order, the existing purchased gas adjustment (PGA) mecha- 
nism needed amending because current practice has lagged behind industry changes. The previous 
requirements for LDC gas purchase contracts, written in the 1970s, required monthly 
review of the contracts, but the good intentions behind the earlier requirements soon were 
nullified by the growing number of long-term purchase contracts. 
The amended requirements, which apply to all purchases beginning Dec. 1, define daily 
gas purchases as those with terms of less than one calendar month. Purchases for more than one 
calendar month are now defined as monthly gas purchases. Purchases that include both daily 
and monthly volumes are defined as combination contracts. Contracts longer than one month 
during a specific heating season or gas injection storage period are defined as seasonal contracts. 
All contracts falling outside the bounds described above are defined as long-term contracts. 
Long-term and seasonal contracts require competitive bidding and must be filed no less 
than 30 days before the requested contract date. The contracts will not take effect until approved 
by the commission or deemed effective 30 days after filing. 
All contracts must be submitted to the commission within 10 days of the contract 
being finalized. Previous requirements of a statement of alternatives and explanation of 
why a certain alternative was selected in a daily, monthly or combination purchase contract 
have been dropped. 
Commission staff will compare daily, monthly and combination contracts with an index 
generally accepted by the industry and determined to be appropriate by KCC. According to the 
order, “Pricing of a contract consistent with an accepted industry index will create a presumption 
of prudence which may be overcome by a showing of facts or circumstances sufficient to 
establish that the price should have been different. The PGA or energy cost adjustment mechanism 
will pass through the price actually paid or determined to be prudent. The index price is to 
be used as a basis of comparison for the initial prudence review, but is not the price to be 
recovered by the utility.” 
The new requirements apply to all new gas purchase contracts and to all existing contracts 
that are renewed, extended or negotiated. 
KCC also said every public utility with an automatic adjustment clause must meet at 
least once a year with commission staff to discuss gas purchase portfolios, purchase plans 
and objectives.                                                           
