NYMEX sets eNYMEX launch 
NYMEX Chairman Daniel Rappaport yes- 
terday said that eNYMEX, the organization’s new 
Internet venture announced in August (GD 5/8), 
should be up and running by late May 2001. 
The for-profit NYMEX subsidiary is designed 
to be a global exchange for forward trading 
and clearing contracts in a range of physical 
commodities, but the e-platform will focus on 
energy and metals initially. 
Rappaport also indicated that NYMEX was 
pursuing private, “strategic” investors to buy 
10%-20% of eNYMEX. 
Meanwhile, NYMEX has set Wednesday as 
the target date for its “demutualization,” a 
changeover that will convert the organization into 
a for-profit structure. Pending legal notification, 
the exchange will be reorganized as a for-profit 
membership organization under Delaware law. 
The announcement follows a private letter 
from the Internal Revenue Service notifying the 
exchange that there would be no tax consequences 
for making the transformation (GD 10/ 
27). The Securities and Exchange Commission, 
the Commodity Futures Trading Commission and 
97.5% of the exchange’s members had already 
approved the demutualization plan, which would 
change NYMEX to NYMEX, Inc. 
The exchange’s plan also provides for the 
formation of a new stock-holding company, 
NYMEX Holdings, Inc., to own all of the economic 
interests and most of the voting control in the new 
for-profit exchange. Each existing NYMEX Division 
membership will be converted into one share 
of common stock in NYMEX Holdings, representing 
equity in the overall organization, while one 
membership in the Exchange will represent trading 
privileges, NYMEX said.             VM 
